          A new series begins here. The previous one was a diagnosis: why strategy execution goes off the rails. This one is a method: how to install a management rhythm that holds. First episode, first question — what exactly is a management rhythm?
        </p>

        <h2>Three ways to start, three ways to stop</h2>

        <p>
          You have probably done at least one of these three things this year. Possibly all three.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The offsite.</strong> Two days away, a facilitator, sticky notes. Everyone leaves with clear ideas and real intent. Six weeks later, the same topics come back in the same meetings.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The action plan.</strong> Twenty actions, named owners, deadlines. The document is good. Nobody reopens it before the next plan.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The working group.</strong> It moves fast for as long as someone is watching. It stops the day attention shifts elsewhere — a customer emergency, a departure, a regulatory deadline.
        </p>

        <p>
          All three work. That is not the problem. They work at launch, and none of them plans for what comes afterwards. <strong>Each one starts strong; none of them plans the next time.</strong>
        </p>

        <h2>Why momentum is lost</h2>

        <p>
          The temptation is to conclude that willpower was lacking, or that the teams did not follow through. That is rarely the case. Three very ordinary mechanics are enough to explain the fade.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>1 — The topic drops off the radar.</strong> Nobody picks it up again on a date everyone knows. It has not been abandoned: it simply comes after. And "after" never arrives, because after, there is always something else.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>2 — Decisions get replayed.</strong> What was settled is written nowhere, or written in minutes nobody rereads. Three months later, the same question is back on the table and gets discussed as if for the first time. The hours spent the first time are lost.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>3 — Urgency goes first.</strong> Urgency comes back every morning. It has a daily appointment with you, and nobody had to put it in the calendar. Whatever has no date gives way to it — mechanically, without anyone making a conscious decision.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">The point to keep</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700">The willpower is there. It is the calendar that decides. A topic without a recurring appointment loses, every single time, against a topic that has one.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>A management rhythm is four things</h2>

        <p>
          A management rhythm is not "more meetings". It is usually the opposite: fewer meetings, but one that does not move. Four markers separate it from a mere series of check-ins.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>① It comes back.</strong> A fixed date in the calendar, including the quiet weeks. The quiet weeks are precisely what makes the difference: a meeting you only honour when things go badly is not a rhythm, it is a crisis cell.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>② It is short.</strong> Thirty minutes is enough. On a busy week, you shorten it — you do not cancel it. A long format is a format you eventually cancel; a short one survives everything.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>③ It is written down.</strong> What was decided fits in three lines, with a name and a date. Not minutes of the meeting: a list of decisions. The distinction matters — minutes record what was said, a decision list records what was settled.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>④ You reread the previous one.</strong> Each session opens with the decisions from the last one. Two minutes. It is the easiest marker to put in place and, by a wide margin, the most discriminating.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">The marker that counts</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700">The first three markers install easily — a date, a duration, a writing format. It is the fourth that turns a series of meetings into a rhythm: without the reread, every session starts from scratch and decisions never accumulate.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Where to start</h2>

        <p>
          If you have already launched a plan, an offsite or a working group, you were right to. You do need a starting point, and those formats do exactly that job: they mobilise, they clarify, they put everyone back in the same room. What is missing is not the momentum. It is what comes back afterwards.
        </p>

        <p>Three questions to locate where you stand:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>The four-minutes test.</strong> Take your last four sets of committee minutes. How many decisions are written in them, with a name and a date?</li>
          <li><strong>The cancellation test.</strong> Over the last eight weeks, how many times has your management check-in been moved or cancelled?</li>
          <li><strong>The reread test.</strong> At your last session, did you start by rereading the decisions from the previous one?</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-400 rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">Our recommendation</p>
          <p class="text-amber-900">One topic, thirty minutes, the same day every week, for eight weeks. Nothing else. Do not try to cover the whole executive committee scope in the first month: pick the topic that costs you the most today, and build the appointment around it. That is where the rhythm takes hold — and once it holds, it spreads to the other topics on its own.</p>
        </div>

        <p>
          That is also what makes this a shorter job than it looks. You do not need to reorganise anything to start: a slot, a topic and one page are enough. The rest comes with repetition.
        </p>

        <h2>What the rest of the series will cover</h2>

